# long64.rc with the Zend VM regenerated as ZEND_VM_KIND_TAILCALL, and nothing
# else changed. It is long64 rather than control85 so the only difference from
# the SHIPPING binary is the threading model.
#
# THE QUESTION: the interpreter currently dispatches every opcode through a
# call/return into the VM machinery. A tail-call VM chains handler to handler
# instead. Nothing in this repo has measured that on wasm.
#
# WHAT IS ALREADY VERIFIED, so the arm is worth the hours:
#
#   - wasm tail calls execute under V8 and DO eliminate stack growth: a
#     hand-assembled return_call module recursed 5,000,000 deep and returned,
#     where the same module using a plain call blew the stack at 100,000.
#   - workerd accepts them. WebAssembly.validate() answers true for a
#     return_call module in the workers lane.
#   - PHP 8.5's zend_vm_gen.php carries the backend: ZEND_VM_KIND_TAILCALL = 5,
#     with its own codegen branches and a _TAILCALL handler-name variant.
#
# THE VM GENERATES: `#define ZEND_VM_KIND ZEND_VM_KIND_TAILCALL` lands in
# Zend/zend_vm_opcodes.h and zend_vm_execute.h carries 4,895 _TAILCALL handlers.
# Verified on a real tree, which is the only place to read it from.
#
# WHAT IS NOT VERIFIED, and the first one is the likely killer:
#
# MEASURED against this emcc, before spending the hours:
#   __has_attribute(preserve_none) = 0   __has_attribute(musttail) = 1
#
# So `ZEND_OPCODE_HANDLER_CCONV`, which is `ZEND_PRESERVE_NONE` under this kind,
# expands to an UNDEFINED IDENTIFIER on wasm32 and every handler fails with
# "expected ';' after top level declarator" -- reproduced on the real macro
# shape. src/build-variant.sh passes `-DZEND_PRESERVE_NONE=` for this arm, which
# compiles clean and needs no php-src patch: zend_portability.h defines that
# macro only behind HAVE_PRESERVE_NONE, which never fires here. The cost is the
# register-pressure win; musttail is untouched and is what emits return_call.
#
# WHAT IS STILL NOT VERIFIED:
#
#   - Whether clang emits the tail call THROUGH THE VM's dispatch. The probe
#     above used a direct call; the VM tail-calls through a function pointer,
#     which has to become return_call_indirect. Check the artifact with
#     `wasm-objdump -d` for return_call, NOT with a benchmark: a few percent
#     either way proves nothing about whether the arm did anything.
#   - Whether it links. No wasm target has been built with these handlers.
#
# HOW THE KIND IS SELECTED: not by --with-vm-kind, which accepts only
# CALL|SWITCH|GOTO|HYBRID and dies on anything else. src/build-variant.sh
# pre-defines ZEND_VM_GEN_KIND=5 instead, which works because the generator
# defaults it behind `if (!defined(...))`. No php-src patch is involved.
#
# WHY THE AUTO-SELECTION NEVER PICKS IT: Zend/zend_vm_opcodes.h gates TAILCALL
# on `__x86_64__ || __aarch64__`. wasm32 is neither, so the shipped tree falls
# through to CALL. That gate is a source condition and not a capability limit,
# which is the whole premise of this arm.
#
# Score it against long64 on the AUTHENTICATED render, and report binary size,
# cold boot, warm invocation CPU and peak memory alongside the render -- a VM
# change moves all of them and a render figure alone hides a size regression.

# TAIL_CALL=1
# ZEND_LONG64=1

PHP_VERSION=8.5

MAIN_MODULE=0
ASYNCIFY=0
OPTIMIZE=2
INITIAL_MEMORY=96MB

WITH_VRZNO=1
WITH_LIBXML=static
WITH_DOM=static
WITH_SIMPLEXML=static
WITH_XML=static
WITH_YAML=static
WITH_ZLIB=static

WITH_CTYPE=1
WITH_FILTER=1
WITH_SESSION=1
WITH_TOKENIZER=1

WITH_BCMATH=0
WITH_CALENDAR=0
WITH_EXIF=0

WITH_ICONV=0
WITH_MBSTRING=0
WITH_ONIGURUMA=0
WITH_SQLITE=0
WITH_GD=0
WITH_LIBPNG=0
WITH_LIBJPEG=0
WITH_FREETYPE=0
WITH_LIBWEBP=0
WITH_LIBZIP=0
WITH_PHAR=0
WITH_XMLREADER=0
WITH_XMLWRITER=0
WITH_INTL=0
WITH_TIDY=0
WITH_SDL=0
WITH_OPENSSL=0
WITH_PDO_PGLITE=0
WITH_PDO_CFD1=0
WITH_WAITLINE=0

# opcache is compiled in; file_cache_only is set at runtime from the worker ini
CONFIGURE_FLAGS+= --enable-opcache
